{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And Jacob loved Rachel.</b> Meaning — not on account of her beauty, but on account of the fact that Rachel was his destined mate." "<b>Or</b> it may be said along the lines of what Amram, of blessed memory, said (Shabbat 25b) regarding the marriage of a Torah scholar — that he must have a beautiful wife as a counterweight to the evil inclination. And even though Jacob had attained the supreme level of one who is stripped of the evil inclination, with all this the Torah teaches a person proper conduct." "<b>I will serve you seven years.</b> This righteous one intended always to subdue himself with seven: also in his bowing down to his brother — seven times (Genesis 33:3). And this is the secret of (Proverbs 24:16) ״שֶׁבַע יִפּוֹל צַדִּיק וָקָם״ — after his fall. Or perhaps he set her value by the fact that he enslaved himself for seven years, which is more than the term of two Hebrew slaves (Exodus 21:2)." "<b>For Rachel your younger daughter.</b> ״Rachel״ — and not Leah; ״your daughter״ — and not another Rachel from the marketplace; ״the younger״ — that you should not switch the names of your daughters and call the elder one Rachel; ״the younger״ — I say, and not the elder, and it will not avail you to change the name and call her Rachel."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:45.279484527Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}